# Adversarial review: `docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md`

**Verdict:** No P0/P1 findings. The design is fundamentally sound: its four properties that justify the register image as the LLVM integration point (section 1), the 23-opcode set, the six boundary opcodes, the fourteen validator rules, the sparse continuation, offset-table identity, and "R4 as oracle" all verify byte-for-byte against source. The findings below are two genuine design flaws and a set of factual/citation errors that would mislead the implementer or the owner.

## P2 — design-correctness findings

**P2-1 · §4.4 (lines 436–453) · the return frame *does* store `live`; the `ReturnFrame` struct drops a field the port target has, and the doc states the opposite as fact**

The doc asserts *"`live` is not stored: it is re-read from the call instruction at `site_pc` when a payload is built."* The source stores it:
```clojure
;; register.cljc:606-612 — the pushed return frame
{:site-pc pc, :return-pc (inc pc), :frames frames,
 :regs (mapv (fn [r] [r (nth registers r)]) live),
 :live live, :dest rd}
```
It is also read *back out of the frame* by the payload builder — `debruijn_register_effects.cljc:163` destructures `{:keys [site-pc return-pc frames regs live dest]}` and `:190-194` compares the frame's `live` against the call instruction's re-derived `live`. So the correct field list is `{site_pc, return_pc, frames, regs, live, dest, store_of?}`; the Rust `ReturnFrame` (§4.4) omits `live`, and the surrounding prose asserts the source doesn't have it.

*Why this matters:* `:live` is in fact derivable (it equals the call's `live` operand, which `live-exact-rule` already recomputes), so dropping it is probably behavior-preserving — but that is a *deliberate divergence* from the port target, not a description of it. The K2 completion criterion (byte-identical payload parity, §10) makes an omitted field a real parity risk.

**Concrete fix:** Add `live: Vec<u16>` to `ReturnFrame`, or — if the optimization is kept — mark it as an explicit divergence (an amendment in §11) with a parity test asserting the re-derived `live` equals the Clojure frame's stored `:live` for every fixture.

**P2-2 · §4.1 dec.2 + §7.4 (lines 343–348, 800–815) · retain/release is placed in the "inline tier," which is `repr(C)`-only — but the refcount is a Rust `Rc` layout detail, and "first word is the refcount" is false**

The doc claims (§4.1 dec.2) *"Every pointer payload points at an object whose first word is a non-atomic reference count."* For `Rc<T>` (and `Arc<T>`), the refcounts sit in an `RcBox` header **before** the pointed-to `value` — the pointer points at the data, and the counts are at a *negative* offset, in a non-`repr(C)` layout. The same §7.4 table puts "retain and release fast paths" in the inline tier, which is defined as operating "over `repr(C)` layouts only" and being reached without depending on "Rust's unspecified type layout." Those two statements contradict: retain/release *inherently* depends on the `Rc` refcount layout, which is exactly the "unspecified Rust layout" the tier split says the inline tier must not touch.

**Concrete fix:** Move retain/release to the out-of-line tier (it touches a Rust-layout header), and correct dec.2 to state the refcount is at a fixed *negative* offset defined by `Rc` (which risk 3 already implicitly acknowledges as ABI, but mis-labels as `repr(C)`). The inline tier then genuinely covers only tag tests / immediates / file load-store.

**P2-3 · §5.3 rule 3 + §2.6 (lines 603–604, 225) · safe-Rust slice indexing panics, and release aborts — so a validator bug is a node-wide crash, not a `Refuse`**

The doc says operands were "validated at load, so the loop performs no bounds check," and that indexing "uses checked slices in the safe crate." In safe Rust, `code[pc]` *always* bounds-checks and **panics** on OOB; there is no error-return form. §2.6 states "release builds abort on panic," and §4.6 states a node runs *many* machines. The "check-then-commit → `Refuse`" rule (§5.3 rule 2) therefore does **not** cover the residual case of a validator bug (or a translator bug emitting an OOB access in un-bounds-checked LLVM IR): one bad image aborts the entire node. The equivalence law (§7.2) bounds the native path for a *correct* translator but is silent on translator bugs, where native code is memory-unsafe rather than a diagnostic.

**Concrete fix:** Use `.get(idx)` mapping `None` to `Refuse` on the interpreter path (so a validator bug degrades to a refusal, not an abort); revisit `panic = "abort"` or add per-machine panic isolation; and add a K0 fixture class that feeds validator-adversarial images to assert the kernel returns `Refuse` rather than panicking.

## P3 — factual / citation errors

**P3-4 · §6.3 item 6 (line 668) · module-kernel method list is wrong.** "the link-install path" does not exist; `IModuleKernel` (`register.cljc:806-875`) actually exposes `link-format`, `spawn-module`, `image-identity`, `image-holds?`, `attach-module` (this is the link/install step), `lift-closure`, `lower-closure`, `gc-roots`, `gc-children`. Fix: name `attach-module` (and the three omitted methods), since Level 2 / K5 ports this seam.

**P3-5 · scattered line-citation errors:**
- §2.4 cites the heap collector at `engine.cljc:1887-1903`; that range is the *design comment*. The collector lives at `1989-2055` (trigger `:cell/new` at `2136-2139`).
- §6.3 item 2 gives one range `engine.cljc:1658-1672` for five functions; that range is `run-loop` only (`check-wait-set` `:1592`, `resume-from-run-queue` `:1733-1765`, `scheduler-round` `:1768-1779`, terminal-outcome check `:1686-1705`).
- §6.2 cites `continuation-payload` at `debruijn_register_effects.cljc:83-119`; it spans `84-120` (off by one).
- §4.1 dec.4 cites `values.cljc:23-24` for sealed refs; the text is at `26-27`, and it says "sealed plain data" that "name table entries," not "plain-data maps."
- §4.1 dec.5 cites `values.cljc:39-62` for equality; `Closure` is `39-72`, `Continuation` is `75-108`, and vector/list sequential equality is **not** in `values.cljc` at all (it is the host default).

**P3-6 · §11 Q5 (line 1130) · "the float64 kind" does not exist, and bigint/ratio are not deferred.** `scalar-classes` (`debruijn_code.cljc:105-106`) declares 14 classes; `:ratio` and `:bigint` are already first-class. There is no `:float64` class — the `float64` carrier (`cbor/float64?`) is folded into `:double` (`host-double?` `:354`). The third host-gated class is `:char`, not "float64."

**P3-7 · §4.1 (line 334) · closure shape is incomplete.** The closure payload is `{:type :closure, :arity, :body-pc, :frames}` with `:store-of` present *only when truthy* (`register.cljc:564-569`). The doc lists `{owner, arity, body_pc, frames, store_of}` — omitting `:type` and treating `store_of` as unconditional.

**P3-8 · §3.2 stmt 2 / §7.5 · "one code layer" tension is an argument, not a citation.** `code-as-tuples.md` §1 states code lives "in one layer only, content." A native artifact is *executable code* outside the content layer. The doc's "projection / derived / recomputed at will" framing satisfies "never an identity" and "no code in a ref," but does not squarely satisfy "one code layer." This is correctly surfaced as owner question 1 (C1/A3), but should be recorded as *unratified*, not honored. (Similarly, §7.2's extension of semantic.md §6.4 "immutability observable only at park points" from the operand stack to register contents is a faithful but unratified generalization.)

---

**Summary:** 3 P2 (return-frame `live` omission, retain/release tier contradiction, panic→abort node-wide DoS) and 5 P3 (module-method names, line-number drift, "float64 kind", closure shape, one-code-layer tension). All are actionable and cite repository evidence; none block the design, and the doc's own §11 already tees up the ratification items (owner questions 1–5) that P2-2 and P3-8 depend on.

No code was modified — this was a read-only review as instructed.
